# The published receipts are JSON, and their `receipt_sha256` covers the PARSED
# claim re-canonicalised -- not the file bytes. That is a deliberate design
# property, not luck: a receipt survives being emailed, pasted into a ticket, or
# checked out on a platform with different line endings, which a raw byte-pin does
# not. `test_a_receipt_survives_line_ending_conversion` pins it.
#
# They are still marked -text. Two byte-pins in this estate were broken by exactly
# this conversion in a single day (a release SHA256SUMS that could not verify on any
# platform, and a ten-file vendored manifest that was red on Windows and green in
# Linux CI). Robustness by design plus robustness by attribute costs nothing; being
# wrong about which one you had costs a trust product its credibility.
stream/** -text

# The vectors and the challenge bundles MOVED into the package so that a `pip install`
# gives a stranger everything the README tells them to run. The rules protecting them
# did not move with them: `vectors/**` and `challenge/**` matched nothing afterwards,
# and `git ls-files --eol` showed the vectors as `i/lf w/crlf attr/` -- silently
# unprotected, and silently converted in the working tree.
#
# A path-based rule is a dangling pointer the moment the path moves, and nothing
# announces it. The CI check that should have caught this had the same stale paths and
# still passed, because it asserted its file list was non-empty rather than asserting
# its COUNT -- stream/ alone kept it green while it checked 5 files of 14.
src/obsign_verify/data/** -text
